{
  // A separate project because these files are workers, not page code: `self` is a
  // `WorkerGlobalScope`, not a `Window`, and the app's tsconfig uses the DOM lib.
  //
  // They are checked at all because the bridge computes heap offsets and pointer values and
  // previously had no static checking of any kind — it was excluded from the app's project
  // along with the rest of `public/`.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "WebWorker"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "strict": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "types": []
  },
  // ONE PROJECT PER BUNDLE. `tsconfig.render.json` is the other one, and the two include
  // different files on purpose: a worker scope is whatever its own bundle concatenated into
  // it, so checking both bundles' sources in one program would declare `KNOWN_OPS` twice and,
  // worse, would type-check a PDFium call inside the bundle that does not load PDFium.
  //
  // `pnpm check` runs both. Adding a file to a bundle means adding it here AND to that
  // bundle's `order` in `tools/stage-web-engines.mjs` -- the same list twice, which
  // `src/worker/bundle-files.test.ts` compares, in order.
  //
  // THIS COMMENT NAMED THE WRONG TEST, and nothing held the two lists together at all. It said
  // `src/production-build.test.ts` did; that test asserts markers inside the BUILT bundles and
  // says nothing about which files went into them. A file added here and not there would have
  // been type-checked and never shipped; the reverse would have SHIPPED UNCHECKED, in the one
  // directory that is type-checked precisely because it computes heap offsets and pointer
  // values. Found by code review.
  "include": [
    "globals.d.ts",
    "globals-render.d.ts",
    "prelude.js",
    "render-prelude.js",
    "bridge-common.js",
    "bridge-pdfium.js",
    "worker-protocol.js",
    "render-main.js"
  ]
}
